WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.526
Draw six members of a discussion service across
three storage partitions.

00:00:04.526 --> 00:00:08.679
Give each member subscriptions to authors on both
other partitions.

00:00:08.679 --> 00:00:14.878
Placing a member's subscription list beside that
member helps discover followed authors locally, but

00:00:14.878 --> 00:00:20.892
it cannot also place every author's canonical posts
beside every subscriber without copying them.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.115
Moving an author to satisfy one reader may make
another reader's path less local.

00:00:26.115 --> 00:00:30.622
The small graph exposes the conflict before any
large-scale benchmark.

00:00:30.622 --> 00:00:32.618
Compare three different copies.

00:00:32.618 --> 00:00:38.927
A replica preserves another copy of the same owned
data for a defined availability or read-serving

00:00:38.927 --> 00:00:39.442
purpose.

00:00:39.442 --> 00:00:43.885
A materialized feed stores a reader-oriented
representation of posts.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.719
A cache retains reusable results under a freshness
policy.

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:52.131
All consume resources, but their ownership and
update rules differ.

00:00:52.131 --> 00:00:56.477
State which operation needs each copy and how stale
it may become.

00:00:56.477 --> 00:01:02.996
Hashing member identifiers can spread ownership, yet
it does not eliminate popular-author hotspots,

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:06.288
cross-partition relationships or network transfer.

00:01:06.288 --> 00:01:10.963
Scaling claims must include those remaining costs
and skewed workloads.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.037
An author in a hypothetical community has one
hundred thousand followers, but only five thousand
of

00:01:17.037 --> 00:01:19.597
them read during a short freshness window.

00:01:19.597 --> 00:01:23.927
Eagerly updating every feed pays for one hundred
thousand destinations.

00:01:23.927 --> 00:01:29.719
Fetching the author's recent posts during those
active readers' requests may do much less work,

00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:32.890
especially if results can be shared through a cache.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:39.199
These illustrative counts motivate a hybrid rather
than a rule that every post must follow the same

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:40.280
publication path.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:46.197
Precompute entries for ordinary authors and merge
selected high-fan-out authors at read time.

00:01:46.197 --> 00:01:52.496
Choose the threshold using observed fan-out cost,
active readership, freshness and cache behaviour,

00:01:52.496 --> 00:01:54.277
not a fixed celebrity label.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.336
The merge still needs stable ordering and
pagination: a post appearing in both paths must not
be

00:02:00.336 --> 00:02:03.040
shown twice, and deletion must affect both.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.505
The storage technology for the exceptional path is a
separate decision.

00:02:07.505 --> 00:02:13.226
This is an architecture exercise about moving work
between reads and writes, not a verified

00:02:13.226 --> 00:02:16.433
description of a current commercial social network.
